  "body": "## Settings fix\r\n\r\n`insecure` and `http-insecure` are the same setting. So are `http-root-ca` and `http-root-cas`, and `grpc-root-ca` and `grpc-root-cas`. Settings stack: the request beats the file and the file beats the environment. That only worked when every layer used the same name.\r\n\r\nSay your `dev` environment turns certificate checks off, and one request turns them back on:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"dev\": {\r\n    \"settings.insecure\": \"true\"\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```http\r\n### Payments health\r\n# @name health\r\n# @setting http-insecure false\r\nGET https://localhost:8443/health\r\n```\r\n\r\nPrevious versions always let `insecure` win, wherever it was set. The certificate was never checked:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nName: health\r\nRequest: GET https://localhost:8443/health\r\nEnvironment: dev\r\nStatus: 200 OK\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow the request-level setting wins, and TLS verification fails as expected::\r\n\r\n```text\r\nErrors:\r\n  Request error:\r\n    error[tls]: request failed\r\n    perform request\r\n    ╰─> Get \"https://localhost:8443/health\"\r\n        ╰─> tls: failed to verify certificate\r\n            ╰─> x509: “localhost” certificate is not trusted\r\n```\r\n\r\nOther places that follow the order now:\r\n\r\n- `$shared` is the lowest layer. `\"settings.http-insecure\"` in an environment overrides `\"settings.insecure\"` in `$shared`.\r\n- An `@apply` patch sits above the request, and a `null` in it removes both names from the request.\r\n- SSE and WebSocket requests use the same rules.\r\n\r\nThe full layer order is on the [HTTP settings](https://resterm.app/docs/http-settings/) page.\r\n\r\n## Both names in one place\r\n\r\nWhen one layer sets both names, Resterm can't know which one you meant, so the request stops with exit code 26:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nerror[protocol]: http-insecure and insecure are the same setting. Use one of them.\r\n```\r\n\r\nBefore, `insecure` would win. The request still runs if a higher layer sets the value, or an `@apply` patch removes it. With grouped environments you'll get the same error when profiles from two groups each set one of the names. The `grpc-` pair only stops gRPC requests.\r\n\r\n## Variables in `proxy`\r\n\r\n```http\r\n# @setting proxy {{proxy.url}}\r\n```\r\n\r\nOlder versions didn't expand this and refused to send the request:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nerror[protocol]: invalid proxy \"{{proxy.url}}\" (use a URL such as http://host:8080)\r\n```\r\n\r\nVariables now work in request-level proxy settings, file defaults, and `settings.proxy` in environment files. If the variable isn't defined, nothing is sent and you get `expand proxy: undefined variable: proxy.url`.\r\n\r\n## Examples\r\n\r\nAll examples are now mirrored on the web: [resterm.app/examples](https://resterm.app/examples/)\r\n\r\n## Upgrading\r\n\r\n1. Look for files and environments that set both `insecure` and `http-insecure` in the same place, or both names of a root CA setting. Keep one.\r\n2. If a request starts failing TLS checks after the update, check its certificate or CA path. That check was being skipped before.\r\n\r\nComing from something older than 1.18.1? Read the [1.18.1](https://github.com/unkn0wn-root/resterm/releases/tag/v1.18.1) and [1.18.0](https://github.com/unkn0wn-root/resterm/releases/tag/v1.18.0) notes.\r\n"
